  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nAs of 2 PM Friday afternoon, an amplified midlevel pattern remains \nacross the CONUS with troughing across the eastern US and ridging \nacross the western half. This particular pattern leaves KS \nwithin a northwest flow regime with the core of the ridge southwest\nof the state across the Four Corners region. A low and midlevel\nbaroclinic zone extends from western SD through portions of \neastern KS. Regional radar reveals a cluster of thunderstorm \nactivity across portions of central and eastern NE. This is \nactivity is being further enhanced by a 40-50 kt midlevel speed \nmax diving southeast across SD/NE. The downstream across \nnorthern KS seems quite unfavorable for storm maintenance with \nconsiderable convective inhibition. Latest mesoanalysis reveals \n150-200 J/kg of inhibition. As a result, short range guidance \nlends little help with how this convection will evolve into KS. \nGiven the lack of well-established, deep-convection combined \nwith the increasing convective inhibition into KS, hedging to a \nmostly dry afternoon/evening across the forecast area. Short \nterm satellite/radar trends will need to be monitored.\n\nThunderstorm chances are expected to increase late tonight into \nSaturday morning as portions of northern/eastern KS remain \nwithin the low/level baroclinic zone. A strengthening LLJ of \n30-35 kt should provide a focal point for additional storm \ndevelopment from western into central NE. Mean storm motions are\nexpected to be towards the southeast, bringing storm chances \ninto portions of northern/eastern KS generally after 2-3 AM. The\nbest storm chances extend generally along/east of I-135. \nElevated instability up to 1500 J/kg combined with effective \nshear of 30-35 kt could support a large hail potential. DCAPE \nvalues of 1000-1200 J/kg would support a damaging wind potential\nas well. As the LLJ weakens Saturday morning, the coverage of \nshower and storm activity should diminish. \n\nThe forecast area will remain within a northwest midlevel flow \nregime into next week, as subtle perturbations eject into Plains on a \nnear-daily occurrence. This will support at least small storm \nchances, especially at night. This includes Saturday night into \nSunday morning as convection develops across CO and gradually moves \neast into the Plains. The lack of obvious forcing for ascent lends \nto low confidence in storm coverage/timing. \n\nIn regards to temperatures, the core of the midlevel ridge will\nremain southwest of the forecast area over the next 7 days. \nThis should keep the hottest temperatures southwest of the state\nwith highs generally in the low to mid 90s each afternoon.",
